The attached image (Figure 11 from the 2015 Journal of Controlled Release paper) illustrates the residual inflammation in skeletal muscle two weeks after intramuscular injection of adjuvants. The left panel, labeled AH (aluminum hydroxide adjuvant), shows extensive and dense granulomatous inflammation (marked by asterisks) within the muscle (M), with the inset detailing the cellular aggregates. In contrast, the right panel, representing Nano-11, exhibits significantly less residual inflammation (asterisk) and fewer macrophages, appearing as slender cords between muscle fibers (M), with the inset showing minimal cellular infiltration. This demonstrates that Nano-11 induces a more transient and rapidly resolving inflammatory response compared to aluminum hydroxide adjuvant.